Since k273, `ocp apply` waits up to 300 s for `nvidia.com/gpu` in status.allocatable after the GPU Operator step; a node that does not get there is a `[WARN]` on STDERR and the apply still succeeds. On AMD (k251) the same situation is a failed component: `— APPLY INCOMPLETE`, exit non-zero. `ocp node add` is non-fatal for both vendors.

Main agent's call for k273 (mine, not the maintainer's): keep NVIDIA at [WARN] for now. Reasons: no live NVIDIA check of the wait yet; the resource name can differ (time-slicing with renameByDefault → nvidia.com/gpu.shared, MIG mixed → nvidia.com/mig-*), so a hard gate could turn green production applies red with no live evidence.

Done = a live run on an NVIDIA node (crag/brain/cortex — only with the maintainer's go) shows what the operator publishes there, then either parity with AMD (failed component, ADR 0017 amended) or the asymmetry recorded in ADR 0017 with its reason.

Live evidence, 2026-11-03, on otho (RTX 3090, driver 610.57.04, amdlab cluster):
- With OCP's own ClusterPolicy (migManager off, no devicePlugin config, so no time-slicing and no renameByDefault), otho publishes status.allocatable nvidia.com/gpu: "1".
- An idempotent ocp apply with image live-20bec47 exited 0 and reported 'GPU Operator up to date'.
- The ClusterPolicy OCP generates sets neither MIG mixed nor time-slicing. The operator's default MIG strategy is single, which publishes MIG slices as nvidia.com/gpu too. So the variants that kept k273 at [WARN] (nvidia.com/gpu.shared, nvidia.com/mig-*) come only from a ClusterPolicy OCP does not write.

Main agent's call (mine, not the maintainer's): parity with AMD. A GPU node without nvidia.com/gpu allocatable after the GPU Operator step fails that component, so the apply reports APPLY INCOMPLETE and exits non-zero. ocp node add stays non-fatal for both vendors. ADR 0017 gets amended to match.

